    <!-- Learn screen -->
    <string name="learn_about_title">About Fechtkarte</string>
    <string name="learn_about_body">Fechtkarte generates warm-up and routine drill cards for Historical European Martial Arts practice — short sequences to run through before or between the real work of a session.</string>
    <string name="learn_how_to_read_title">How to read a card</string>
    <string name="learn_how_to_read_notation">The notation is adapted from Joachim Meyer\'s diagrams in the Lund manuscript, his later fencing treatise. Each numbered circle marks the starting point of a cut, which travels along its line through the centre to the opening opposite. Work through the numbers in sequence — one, then two, and so on.</string>
    <string name="learn_how_to_read_thrust">A dot beneath a numeral means thrust instead of cut, from that same starting point.</string>
    <string name="learn_how_to_read_branching">Where lines branch, the choice is yours — the notation fixes the opening a cut starts from, not the path it takes to get there.</string>
    <string name="learn_how_to_read_colour">The card\'s background colour tells you which hand leads: the two shades alternate a drill between right and left hand, card to card. Edge, guard, footwork and tempo are left to you — the card only fixes the sequence of openings, not how you get there.</string>
    <string name="learn_how_to_read_repetitions">A reasonable way to work through a card: about fifty repetitions, or five minutes, before moving on to the next one.</string>
    <string name="learn_worked_example_title">Worked example</string>
    <string name="learn_worked_example_intro">A right-hand card with three actions, the second a thrust:</string>
    <string name="learn_open_source_notices">Open-source notices</string>

